steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1788801402
      nanos: 15714735
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "You are the Senior Product Designer + Senior Frontend Engineer responsible for implementing the PRIMARY INVESTIGATION PAGE of Asheilder.\n\nIMPORTANT: We want an EXACT, pixel-level reproduction of the provided reference image for the Investigation Page.\n\nDo NOT redesign it.\nDo NOT \342\200\234improve\342\200\235 the visual layout.\nDo NOT introduce your own UI patterns.\nDo NOT change the existing Asheilder visual language unnecessarily.\n\nThe reference image is the source of truth for the Investigation Page UI.\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nBuild the Investigation Page as the central workspace where investigators ingest an email and start a complete forensic investigation.\n\nThe investigation flow must be:\n\nINGEST \342\206\222 PLAN \342\206\222 INVESTIGATE \342\206\222 RESULTS\n\nThe page should feel like a serious enterprise cybersecurity / digital-forensics product: compact, information-dense, clean, professional, and operational.\n\nFIRST: INSPECT THE EXISTING SYSTEM\n\nBefore writing or modifying code:\n\n1. Inspect the complete frontend repository.\n2. Understand:\n\n   * existing routes\n   * application shell\n   * sidebar/navigation\n   * header\n   * typography\n   * spacing system\n   * colors\n   * components\n   * icons\n   * buttons\n   * modals/dialogs\n   * cards\n   * tables\n   * state management\n   * API/service layer\n   * existing investigation-related components\n3. Identify the exact framework, UI library, styling system, and component conventions already being used.\n4. Reuse existing components wherever possible.\n5. Do NOT create duplicate components when an equivalent component already exists.\n6. Do NOT modify backend logic, database schemas, authentication, ML models, email parsing, threat-analysis logic, or APIs unless absolutely required by an existing frontend contract.\n7. Preserve every existing working feature.\n\nThen inspect the provided reference image carefully.\n\nREFERENCE IMAGE = SOURCE OF TRUTH\n\nReproduce the reference image as closely as technically possible:\n\n* overall page structure\n* layout proportions\n* content hierarchy\n* panel positioning\n* spacing\n* typography\n* font weights\n* border radius\n* borders\n* shadows\n* icons\n* button dimensions\n* alignment\n* whitespace\n* modal placement\n* navigation\n* responsive behavior\n* visual density\n\nDo not approximate the design if the existing image clearly shows the intended structure.\n\nPRIMARY INVESTIGATION EXPERIENCE\n\nThis page is NOT a generic upload page.\n\nIt is the main forensic investigation workspace.\n\nThe investigator should be able to:\n\n1. Ingest an email\n2. Review the ingested evidence\n3. Start an investigation\n4. See the investigation plan/progress\n5. Navigate toward forensic results\n\nINGEST\n\nThe primary action must allow investigators to provide email evidence.\n\nSupport the existing system\'s supported ingestion methods, including:\n\n* Upload `.eml`\n* Gmail integration\n* Microsoft / Outlook integration\n* Email ingestion from the existing backend/API if already implemented\n\nThe top-right investigation action must follow the reference design.\n\nWhen the investigator chooses the email ingestion action:\n\n* open the appropriate modal/dialog\n* keep the modal visually consistent with the reference image\n* allow Gmail integration\n* allow Microsoft/Outlook integration\n* allow `.eml` upload\n* show clear ingestion state\n* show selected/ingested email information\n* prevent accidental duplicate submissions\n* provide clear loading/error/success states\n\nDo not create fake integrations if the backend does not support them.\n\nUse existing API contracts.\n\nIf an integration is unavailable, keep the UI ready for the existing/future contract without inventing backend behavior.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter successful ingestion, the system should transition naturally into the investigation planning stage.\n\nThe investigator should be able to understand:\n\n* what evidence was ingested\n* what investigation is about to run\n* which analysis stages will execute\n* current investigation status\n\nThe planning stage should visually fit the reference image and existing Asheilder design.\n\nINVESTIGATION\n\nThe investigation should represent the existing Asheilder forensic pipeline.\n\nConceptually:\n\nRaw Email\n\342\206\222 Email Parsing\n\342\206\222 Header Analysis\n\342\206\222 Authentication Analysis\n\342\206\222 Received Chain / IP Analysis\n\342\206\222 URL Extraction\n\342\206\222 Attachment Analysis\n\342\206\222 Domain / IP Intelligence\n\342\206\222 Threat Intelligence\n\342\206\222 Phishing / BEC Analysis\n\342\206\222 Correlation\n\342\206\222 Risk Assessment\n\342\206\222 Forensic Findings\n\nDo NOT implement duplicate backend analysis logic in the frontend.\n\nThe frontend should consume the existing backend results/state.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nFollow this implementation order:\n\nPHASE 1 \342\200\224 INGEST\nUnderstand and connect the existing email ingestion mechanisms.\n\nPHASE 2 \342\200\224 PLAN\nRepresent the investigation plan and investigation state.\n\nPHASE 3 \342\200\224 IMPLEMENT\nImplement the reference UI using existing Asheilder components and conventions.\n\nPHASE 4 \342\200\224 INTEGRATE\nConnect the page to existing routes, APIs, state, authentication, and application shell.\n\nPHASE 5 \342\200\224 VERIFY\nCompare the implementation against the reference image and fix visual discrepancies.\n\nDESIGN RULES\n\nThe page must remain compact.\n\nAvoid unnecessary:\n\n* giant hero sections\n* oversized cards\n* excessive padding\n* decorative gradients\n* excessive animations\n* unnecessary illustrations\n* huge empty areas\n* generic dashboard widgets\n* marketing-style UI\n\nThis is an investigation console, not a landing page.\n\nMaintain high information density without making the interface cluttered.\n\nRESPONSIVE BEHAVIOR\n\nThe page must work correctly across:\n\n* laptop\n* desktop\n* large desktop\n* smaller screens\n\nDo NOT simply scale the desktop layout down.\n\nPreserve the reference composition and information hierarchy.\n\nThe application should not develop huge unused whitespace when entering fullscreen or using wider monitors.\n\nUse responsive containers, flex/grid behavior, and sensible max-width rules.\n\nIMPORTANT: Existing Asheilder pages should remain visually consistent.\n\nDO NOT BREAK EXISTING SYSTEM\n\nBefore finishing, verify:\n\n* existing navigation still works\n* existing routes still work\n* authentication still works\n* existing API calls still work\n* dashboard remains unchanged\n* existing components are not broken\n* no unrelated files are modified unnecessarily\n* no backend behavior is changed\n* no mock data replaces real data\n* no hardcoded investigation results are introduced\n* no console errors\n* no TypeScript/build errors\n* no broken imports\n* no missing assets\n\nCODE QUALITY\n\nKeep the implementation modular.\n\nPrefer:\n\n* reusable components\n* existing design tokens\n* existing UI primitives\n* existing API clients\n* existing state management\n* existing routing\n\nAvoid:\n\n* duplicated code\n* unnecessary dependencies\n* giant monolithic components\n* inline hacks\n* arbitrary pixel values when design tokens already exist\n* introducing another UI framework\n\nDo not install a new library simply to reproduce something that can already be implemented with the project\'s existing stack.\n\nVISUAL QA\n\nAfter implementation, perform a deliberate visual comparison with the reference image.\n\nCheck:\n\n* x/y positioning\n* widths/heights\n* spacing\n* typography\n* icon sizing\n* button sizing\n* borders\n* radius\n* alignment\n* modal dimensions\n* sidebar proportions\n* content density\n* responsive behavior\n\nIf something differs from the reference image, fix it.\n\nDo not rationalize the difference as a \342\200\234design improvement.\342\200\235\n\nFINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nThe final result should look like the reference image belongs natively inside Asheilder.\n\nIt must NOT feel like a newly designed page placed beside the existing system.\n\nThe Investigation Page is the PRIMARY forensic workflow of Asheilder, so prioritize:\n\n1. Reference-image fidelity\n2. Existing system consistency\n3. Real ingestion integration\n4. Clear INGEST \342\206\222 PLAN \342\206\222 INVESTIGATE flow\n5. Responsive compact layout\n6. Production-quality implementation\n\nWORKING METHOD:\n\nINSPECT \342\206\222 UNDERSTAND \342\206\222 PLAN \342\206\222 IMPLEMENT \342\206\222 INTEGRATE \342\206\222 VISUALLY COMPARE \342\206\222 FIX \342\206\222 VERIFY\n\nDo not start coding immediately.\n\nFirst inspect the repository and existing architecture, identify what can be reused, formulate the implementation plan, and only then implement.\n\nDo not stop after creating the visual shell. The page must be properly integrated into the existing Asheilder application."
    items {
      text: "You are the Senior Product Designer + Senior Frontend Engineer responsible for implementing the PRIMARY INVESTIGATION PAGE of Asheilder.\n\nIMPORTANT: We want an EXACT, pixel-level reproduction of the provided reference image for the Investigation Page.\n\nDo NOT redesign it.\nDo NOT \342\200\234improve\342\200\235 the visual layout.\nDo NOT introduce your own UI patterns.\nDo NOT change the existing Asheilder visual language unnecessarily.\n\nThe reference image is the source of truth for the Investigation Page UI.\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nBuild the Investigation Page as the central workspace where investigators ingest an email and start a complete forensic investigation.\n\nThe investigation flow must be:\n\nINGEST \342\206\222 PLAN \342\206\222 INVESTIGATE \342\206\222 RESULTS\n\nThe page should feel like a serious enterprise cybersecurity / digital-forensics product: compact, information-dense, clean, professional, and operational.\n\nFIRST: INSPECT THE EXISTING SYSTEM\n\nBefore writing or modifying code:\n\n1. Inspect the complete frontend repository.\n2. Understand:\n\n   * existing routes\n   * application shell\n   * sidebar/navigation\n   * header\n   * typography\n   * spacing system\n   * colors\n   * components\n   * icons\n   * buttons\n   * modals/dialogs\n   * cards\n   * tables\n   * state management\n   * API/service layer\n   * existing investigation-related components\n3. Identify the exact framework, UI library, styling system, and component conventions already being used.\n4. Reuse existing components wherever possible.\n5. Do NOT create duplicate components when an equivalent component already exists.\n6. Do NOT modify backend logic, database schemas, authentication, ML models, email parsing, threat-analysis logic, or APIs unless absolutely required by an existing frontend contract.\n7. Preserve every existing working feature.\n\nThen inspect the provided reference image carefully.\n\nREFERENCE IMAGE = SOURCE OF TRUTH\n\nReproduce the reference image as closely as technically possible:\n\n* overall page structure\n* layout proportions\n* content hierarchy\n* panel positioning\n* spacing\n* typography\n* font weights\n* border radius\n* borders\n* shadows\n* icons\n* button dimensions\n* alignment\n* whitespace\n* modal placement\n* navigation\n* responsive behavior\n* visual density\n\nDo not approximate the design if the existing image clearly shows the intended structure.\n\nPRIMARY INVESTIGATION EXPERIENCE\n\nThis page is NOT a generic upload page.\n\nIt is the main forensic investigation workspace.\n\nThe investigator should be able to:\n\n1. Ingest an email\n2. Review the ingested evidence\n3. Start an investigation\n4. See the investigation plan/progress\n5. Navigate toward forensic results\n\nINGEST\n\nThe primary action must allow investigators to provide email evidence.\n\nSupport the existing system\'s supported ingestion methods, including:\n\n* Upload `.eml`\n* Gmail integration\n* Microsoft / Outlook integration\n* Email ingestion from the existing backend/API if already implemented\n\nThe top-right investigation action must follow the reference design.\n\nWhen the investigator chooses the email ingestion action:\n\n* open the appropriate modal/dialog\n* keep the modal visually consistent with the reference image\n* allow Gmail integration\n* allow Microsoft/Outlook integration\n* allow `.eml` upload\n* show clear ingestion state\n* show selected/ingested email information\n* prevent accidental duplicate submissions\n* provide clear loading/error/success states\n\nDo not create fake integrations if the backend does not support them.\n\nUse existing API contracts.\n\nIf an integration is unavailable, keep the UI ready for the existing/future contract without inventing backend behavior.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter successful ingestion, the system should transition naturally into the investigation planning stage.\n\nThe investigator should be able to understand:\n\n* what evidence was ingested\n* what investigation is about to run\n* which analysis stages will execute\n* current investigation status\n\nThe planning stage should visually fit the reference image and existing Asheilder design.\n\nINVESTIGATION\n\nThe investigation should represent the existing Asheilder forensic pipeline.\n\nConceptually:\n\nRaw Email\n\342\206\222 Email Parsing\n\342\206\222 Header Analysis\n\342\206\222 Authentication Analysis\n\342\206\222 Received Chain / IP Analysis\n\342\206\222 URL Extraction\n\342\206\222 Attachment Analysis\n\342\206\222 Domain / IP Intelligence\n\342\206\222 Threat Intelligence\n\342\206\222 Phishing / BEC Analysis\n\342\206\222 Correlation\n\342\206\222 Risk Assessment\n\342\206\222 Forensic Findings\n\nDo NOT implement duplicate backend analysis logic in the frontend.\n\nThe frontend should consume the existing backend results/state.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nFollow this implementation order:\n\nPHASE 1 \342\200\224 INGEST\nUnderstand and connect the existing email ingestion mechanisms.\n\nPHASE 2 \342\200\224 PLAN\nRepresent the investigation plan and investigation state.\n\nPHASE 3 \342\200\224 IMPLEMENT\nImplement the reference UI using existing Asheilder components and conventions.\n\nPHASE 4 \342\200\224 INTEGRATE\nConnect the page to existing routes, APIs, state, authentication, and application shell.\n\nPHASE 5 \342\200\224 VERIFY\nCompare the implementation against the reference image and fix visual discrepancies.\n\nDESIGN RULES\n\nThe page must remain compact.\n\nAvoid unnecessary:\n\n* giant hero sections\n* oversized cards\n* excessive padding\n* decorative gradients\n* excessive animations\n* unnecessary illustrations\n* huge empty areas\n* generic dashboard widgets\n* marketing-style UI\n\nThis is an investigation console, not a landing page.\n\nMaintain high information density without making the interface cluttered.\n\nRESPONSIVE BEHAVIOR\n\nThe page must work correctly across:\n\n* laptop\n* desktop\n* large desktop\n* smaller screens\n\nDo NOT