    thinking: "**Analyzing the Light Theme**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the light theme observed in the reference image. The modern, bright, and enterprise-focused aesthetic is strikingly clear. I\'m focusing on the sidebar background to capture its design elements effectively.\n\n\n**Exploring the Color Palette**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics. The pure white or near-white background (likely `bg-white` or similar) is crucial. The Ashielder logo is a focus, particularly the shield with the blue checkmark, which is just like the current logo. The menu items, I think, are very straightforward, with an active state to emphasize the \"Investigation\" section. A light grayish-blue is expected for the card at the sidebar\'s bottom.\n\n\n**Dissecting the Details**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the finer points. The sidebar footer presents an interesting design with its AI-powered text and subtle Indian map silhouette. I\'m focusing on the top bar\'s notification bell and avatar section with the \"TD\" initial and chevron. I\'m looking at the breadcrumb trail now and I am capturing the styling of the text. The main content area\'s off-white background and breadcrumb design are also being carefully examined for style.\n\n\n**Examining the Add Email Button**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the \"Add Email\" button and its associated dropdown. The blue button\'s rounded design and hover/click behavior are key, along with the dropdown\'s card style. The dropdown menu will have white cards with rounded edges and shadow and border. I\'m noting the specific icons and text styling for the Gmail, Microsoft, and upload options. The icons include standard Google Workspace M and Microsoft icons and document. The text includes bold titles, a description, and their respective colors.\n\n\n**Detailing the Start Investigation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Start Investigation\" section\'s design. The large container card\'s white background and subtle dashed border, specifically the `border-2 border-dashed border-blue-100` effect, are of interest. The rounded padding is evident. I\'m focusing on the circular icon and text within the container. I see a light blue circle, and I am studying the text styling and sizes.\n\n\n**Defining the Investigation Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the \"Start Investigation\" card details, specifically the structure of the cards that help the user select the workflow. I\'m noting the text styles, like the blue-500 class for the icons and the different text classes for heading and subheading. I\'m focusing on the Gmail, Microsoft, and upload card options that use the icon, title, and subtitle system, and I am studying the details.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Workflow Cards**\n\nI\'m now refining the breakdown of the workflow selection cards. I\'ve documented the exact styling of the four cards, specifically the text sizes, colors, and layout. I\'ve detailed the \'or\' divider and a large dropzone with an upload icon, which will provide the option for the user to upload a file to start an investigation. The dropzone\'s subtle styling has also been recorded.\n\n\n**Defining the Drag & Drop**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the drag-and-drop file upload. I\'ve noted the gray cloud outline, and the instructional text. I\'ve broken down the \"Recent Investigations\" card\'s elements, including its white background, rounded edges, subtle border, and shadow. I\'m noting the card header\'s clock icon and text and the right-aligned \"View All\" link.\n\n\n**Cataloging the Table Styles**\n\nI\'m now detailing the table of \"Recent Investigations\". I am noting the exact font and text styles for each column header including the colors and size. The row data is also being cataloged, I\'m focusing on the bold text for the subjects. I am making sure to note the various colored pill-shaped tags to indicate status. The date and actions are also accounted for, especially the actions\' 3-dot icon. I see and am making sure to record all visual characteristics.\n\n\n**Defining the Recent Investigations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Recent Investigations\" table rows. I\'m focusing on the formatting, specifically the bolded subject text, the icons and text of the source column, the pill-shaped status tags (both the soft green and the soft amber/yellow variations), and the risk score pills. The specific date formats and action icons are also being recorded. I see these represent some of the most critical states of each investigation.\n\nI\'m now reviewing the next steps and confirming the desired flow: INGEST \342\206\222 PLAN \342\206\222 INVESTIGATE \342\206\222 RESULTS. I am working to keep the page\'s design consistent with a serious enterprise cybersecurity product, ensuring it feels compact, information-dense, and clean. I am also working on an email ingestion action, so I need to open the modal/dialog that is consistent with the image provided, and allow Gmail, Microsoft/Outlook, and .eml upload options. I\'ll need to show clear ingestion state and selected options.\n\n\n**Reviewing Navigation Flows**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the app\'s navigation and routing, specifically how the \"Investigation\" section fits into the existing structure. I\'m noting the changes needed to the sidebar menu labels and route mapping to align with the reference image. The active sidebar item should be \"Investigation\" when on `/investigations` or `/investigation`. I\'m comparing the existing routes (e.g., `/overview`, `/inbox`, `/case/:id`, `/ingest`) with the reference image\'s suggested flow (Dashboard, Investigation, Threat Intelligence, Reports, etc.) to ensure a smooth transition and user experience.\n\n\n**Mapping the Navigation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on navigation routing. I\'ve confirmed `/ingest`, `/investigations`, and `/investigation` all lead to the Investigation Page. I\'m updating the sidebar in `Sidebar.tsx` to match the reference image. I\'ve reviewed the existing routes and am now making adjustments. I\'m focusing on ensuring `/` or `/investigations` or `/investigation` goes to the Investigation Page.\n\n\n"
