  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining India\'s Data**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that \"India\" exists in `worldMapData.json` with the ID \'356\'. I can directly integrate its vector path into the sidebar\'s \"Safer Institutions, Stronger India\" card. I\'m now conducting a detailed examination of the reference image to understand its elements.\n\n\n**Adapting The Header**\n\nOkay, I\'m focusing on the top navigation bar. I\'ve noted the bell icon and user avatar (`TD`) with the chevron in the reference image. The key difference is the absence of the CDGI SOC switcher and large search bar in this view, which I think points to the header adapting. Now I\'m comparing the reference image with `AppTopBar.tsx`, considering the breadcrumbs and title are in the main content. I\'m exploring how the top bar can be styled to align with the `/investigation` or `/investigations` routes, and to match the reference.\n\n\n**Refining UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the top navigation bar and main content structure. I\'m focusing on the bell icon, user avatar, breadcrumbs (`Home > Investigation`), and the blue \"+ Add Email \342\226\276\" button. I\'ve begun outlining the dropdown menu for the button, including options for Gmail, Microsoft, .EML file uploads, and sending emails. Now, I\'m working to describe the h1 title and subtitle and the rounded investigation box\'s elements, including the four cards that allow connecting various mail services or uploading files.\n\n\n**Defining Investigation Actions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the dropdown menu for the \"+ Add Email\" button and outlining the \"Recent Investigations\" card. The menu will feature connecting Gmail and Microsoft accounts, uploading .EML files, or sending an email. I\'m designing the dashed upload box with its cloud icon and the \"Recent Investigations\" card with its table, including column definitions, and mock data for demonstration.\n\n\n**Outlining Investigation Workflow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Recent Investigations\" table, incorporating an example case: \"Invoice #78291\" with specific data points like source, status, risk score, and date. I\'m focusing on the row actions: opening the case detail or quick peek on click, and the three-dot menu with options to view the investigation, re-analyze, export the report, or copy the case ID. This directly addresses the case data presentation and interaction design. I am also planning the investigation flow, from ingestion to results, as outlined in the objective. I am sketching the plan, investigation, and results stages based on the Asheilder design. Finally, I\'m working to describe the required actions of the \"Send Email to Server\" button.\n\n\n**Defining Ingestion Strategies**\n\nI am now focusing on data ingestion strategies. I\'m considering forwarding address options like `ingest@soc.ashielder.in` and emphasizing intact header preservation (RFC 822/ full headers/ as attachment). I am outlining a custom token for unique forwarding. I\'m planning to simulate test forwards, and I\'m developing detailed instructions.\n\nI\'m now detailing integration specifics for Gmail and Microsoft, including modals with authentication flows and sample message selections. I will be incorporating email samples for visual previews. I\'m adding options for selecting ingested messages, or direct OAuth connections. I am also working on \"Start Forensic Investigation\" buttons.\n\n\n**Defining Ingestion Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing the ingestion and investigation flow after uploading an EML file. I\'m focusing on the file picker, metadata display (filename, size, headers, SHA-256), and analysis depth options (L0, L1, L2). I\'m planning the \"Start Investigation\" trigger for the INGEST, PLAN, INVESTIGATE, and RESULTS stages. I\'m also planning the modal for the initial file review and data validation.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Investigation Pipeline**\n\nNow, I\'m visualizing the \"PLAN\" and \"INVESTIGATION\" stages of the investigation. The plan outlines parsing, analysis (authentication, geolocation, URL extraction), and threat intelligence. The investigation stage shows real-time pipeline execution, mapping to Asheilder\'s design, from raw email to URL analysis.\n\n\n**Defining Investigation Results**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the final results of the investigation. The \"RESULTS\" stage includes the final verdict, risk score with confidence level, and assigned case ID. It also displays the top forensic findings, providing a clear path to open the full workspace or return to the investigation hub. I\'m also planning the presentation of the \"Recent Investigations\" table, incorporating the example cases and how I\'ll display the data.\n\n\n**Reviewing Sidebar Implementation**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Recent Investigations\" table, and I have finalized the row actions. I have mock-detailed data presentation with an example case, and now I\'m working on navigation, to `/inbox` (Case Inbox), as well as a new, reactive case appearing at the top of \"Recent Investigations,\" persisted in `casesStore`. I have also started to review the sidebar.\n\n\n**Analyzing UI Mode**\n\nI\'m now detailing the UI mode. I\'ve noted the consistent dark background across modes, which is why the background color needs adjustment. I\'m focusing on the sidebar background, active item, and bottom card to match the clean, light enterprise mode. I will also be ensuring the `AppTopBar` matches this visual style for `/investigation` or `/investigations` routes.\n\n\n**Confirming Theme Implementation**\n\nI\'ve verified the light and dark mode toggle, and confirmed the default theme. I\'ve updated the `getInitialTheme` function to default to \'light\' for a perfect initial visual match. I\'m now examining the `Sidebar.tsx` to align with the chosen light/dark mode, specifying distinct styling for each, specifically the background color, active navigation item, and inactive navigation items.\n\n\n**Refining Routing Logic**\n\nI\'m now detailing the route handling within `App.tsx`. I\'m confirming the `Investigation` page route, noting the breadcrumb as `Home > Investigation`. I\'m adjusting the route handling and clarifying the `Sidebar.tsx` active item to align the investigation routes with the application\'s intended logic.\n\n\n**Mapping Route Navigation**\n\nI\'m now detailing route-specific UI rendering in `App.tsx`. I\'m confirming that `/investigation`, `/investigations`, and `/ingest` render `InvestigationPage`.  I\'m also ensuring the correct components are displayed for routes like `/hunting` and the default route `/overview`, and adjusting the application\'s overall route logic to accommodate user interactions. The `AppTopBar` and `Sidebar` are also adjusted for `/investigation` and `/investigations` now.\n\n\n**Defining Default Route**\n\nI\'ve decided that if a user loads the app on the base route or navigates to `/investigation`, the application *must* default to the `/investigation` route, and show that exact reference image, which will greatly improve the user experience. The app\'s overall route logic is being adjusted to accommodate for this behavior. I\'m focusing on UI elements to ensure route logic implementation.\n\n\n**Defining Sidebar Focus**\n\nI\'m now detailing the sidebar\'s implementation and confirming it reflects the reference image. The sidebar will maintain its width (around 240px) and the logo will include the blue shield with a white checkmark. I\'m focusing on the navigation links (\"Dashboard\" and \"Investigation\"), which are essential for route-specific display. I\'m prioritizing the visual styling of each element to ensure it\'s accurate and responsive.\n\n\n**Refining Sidebar Elements**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating the sidebar design elements. I am adding \"Threat Intelligence,\" \"Reports,\" \"Evidence Vault,\" and \"Settings\" links, with appropriate icons. I\'m focusing on the bottom card, the \"AI-Powered Email\" text and the India map vector graphic to align the design. I\'m also ensuring the background color for elements, and the text colors, match the visual theme.\n\n\n**Styling Action Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Add Email\" button\'s dropdown. It will have options to connect Gmail, Microsoft accounts, upload `.EML` files, and send emails, with clear visual separation. This dropdown will complement the breadcrumb and heading to match `/investigation` and `/investigations` routes. I\'m focused on the button styles for a consistent look.\n\n\n**Styling Dropdown Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the button\'s dropdown elements, including Gmail, Microsoft, EML upload, and a \"send to server\" option, with icons and details for each action, and distinct visual separation. The focus is on ensuring the consistent button and dropdown styles align with the `/investigation` route\'s theme.\n\n\n\n**Detailing Email Ingestion**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Start an Investigation\" section, starting with a container card featuring a light blue/slate dashed border and a circular badge with a blue mail icon.  I\'m focusing on the \"Start an Investigation\" heading and its content. I\'m also styling the title, subtitle, and the central ingestion area based on the designs from the reference.\n\n\n\n**Describing Ingestion Actions**\n\nI\'m now describing each email ingestion action. I am detailing the containers and styling for the individual cards: \"Connect Gmail\" with a multi-colored Gmail icon, \"Connect Microsoft\" with a multi-colored Microsoft icon, an \".EML File Upload\" with a file icon, and a \"Send Email\" action with an email icon. I\'m focusing on text and visual styles that are consistent.\n\n\n**Defining Card Interaction**\n\nI\'m now detailing each individual email ingestion card, and their respective actions. Each has a consistent style, with its icon, title, and subtitle, and these are being adapted for visual cohesion within the \"/investigation\" route. I\'m building out each card, including the title and subtitle, and styling them to match the reference design.\n\n\n**Refining Upload Elements**\n\nI\'m now refining the upload area\'s drag-and-drop element, including the visual divider and the \"browse files\" option. I\'m focusing on the border, icon, and text elements, ensuring they align with the design specifications and are responsive across different screen sizes. I\'m also preparing to add the upload cloud icon.\n\n\n**Defining Investigation UI**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Recent Investigations\" section at the bottom, focusing on its card wrapper and header. I\'m styling its table, including column headers and row styling. I\'m focusing on the \"View All \342\206\222\" action and the clock outline icon, with headers including Subject, Source, Status, Risk Score, Date and action. The row one will have Urgent text to denote important items.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Table Behavior**\n\nI\'m now detailing the styling of the table for \"Recent Investigations,\" particularly focusing on its visual cues. I\'ve noted specific elements like the Gmail and Microsoft icons, the \"Malicious\" and \"Clean\" status pills, the Risk Score badge, and the date format. I\'m also reviewing the action elements, the Subject column, and ensuring consistency across all rows. I am making sure the layout and the urgent styling on the first row are correctly rendered.\n\n\n\n**Styling Risk Table**\n\nI\'m now detailing each individual row of the \"Recent Investigations\" table, adding specific styling for different states. I\'m incorporating icons, risk score badges, status pills, and the \"Urgent\" marker for important entries. I am making sure the layout renders correctly, including all the correct elements and their states, such as the `Malicious` and `Suspicious` status, and varying risk score badges.\n\n\n**Defining Ingestion Modal Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the ingestion modal flow, including Gmail, Microsoft, EML upload, and send-to-server options. Each triggers a unique state: Gmail and Microsoft show account connection status, recent emails, or ID paste options. EML uploads parse headers and display metadata. Send-to-server provides an endpoint, token and handshake specimen.\n\n\n**Visualizing Investigation Pipeline**\n\nI\'m now detailing the ingestion flow after selecting and validating evidence. This includes displaying a summary of the evidence, checking for duplicates, and outlining the forensic pipeline, including its 12 stages, execution budget, and SLA boundary. I\'m also preparing for the execution of the investigation.\n\n\n"
